# Copy this file to `.env` in this directory, fill the required values below, then:
#   docker compose up -d && docker compose ps
#
# Nothing here has a working default, on purpose: a filled-in sample in a public repository
# is a working credential, not a sample. Each ${…:?} guard in docker-compose.yml fires on
# ABSENCE AND ON AN EMPTY VALUE — measured; that second half is the only reason an unedited
# copy of this file is refused at all, since every required line below ships empty. So `up`
# refuses and names the one value it wants; fill that one, run again, and the next names
# itself. Which one comes first is not fixed — treat it as a chain, not a checklist.
#
# A `$` in any value here is an interpolation, not a character: Compose expands `${NAME}`
# and `$NAME` inside this file, and an undefined name expands to NOTHING. A password like
# `Vk5$mT8pQr2zXn6w` reaches the container as `Vk5`, with no warning from anything. Double
# it (`$$`) for one literal `$`, or generate a value that has none — `openssl rand -base64`
# never emits one.

# ── Required ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Any strong password you choose. Used twice from this one line: it seeds the PostgreSQL
# container and is what the app logs in to it with, so the two cannot drift apart.
#   openssl rand -base64 24
DB_PASSWORD=

# Token signing key, minimum 32 bytes. The app refuses to start on a shorter value, and by
# name on the placeholder values this project has published — so generate your own:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=

# Your own address. This becomes the first system administrator, created on startup and
# active immediately — there is no email verification step for it. Public self-registration
# is closed on a self-hosted install, so this is the only way in.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=

# A password you choose — not one from any documentation, and not this variable's own name.
# Maximum 72 UTF-8 BYTES: that is a BCrypt limit rather than ours, so it is 72 ASCII
# characters but only ~36 Cyrillic, ~24 CJK. The app refuses a longer one at startup and
# says so. Changing it later does NOT change an account that already exists.
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ── Optional: where this instance is reached ───────────────────────

# Leave APP_BASE_URL on http://localhost:PORT until a TLS proxy is in front of it: with an
# https base the session cookie is Secure and will not survive plain HTTP, so an https
# value requires actually serving HTTPS.
#APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
# Host port. Change it if 8080 is already taken — the symptom is
# `Bind for 0.0.0.0:8080 failed: port is already allocated`.
# CHANGE APP_BASE_URL ABOVE TO MATCH. Nothing derives one from the other, and nothing
# complains: the instance is healthy while every verification and password-reset link it
# mails points at a port nothing serves, which nobody discovers until a user clicks one.
#APP_PORT=8080
# Bind address for that port. 0.0.0.0 (the default) publishes it on every interface; on a
# public server with no TLS proxy yet, set 127.0.0.1 and reach it over an SSH tunnel.
#APP_BIND=0.0.0.0

# ── Optional: behind a reverse proxy ───────────────────────────────

# Every per-sender budget in the app keys on the client address, and behind a proxy every
# request arrives from the proxy — so one busy tenant can exhaust a bound that was meant to
# be per person, and the symptom is false 429s for everybody. Turn this on when a proxy is
# in front, and ONLY if that proxy overwrites a client-supplied `X-Forwarded-For` (the
# bundled Caddy does): if it passes one through, this hands every caller a free bound by
# letting them choose their own identity. The app trusts the right-most entry.
#RATE_LIMIT_TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=false

# ── Optional: which build runs ─────────────────────────────────────

# The release LINE, so patch releases arrive on `docker compose pull`. Never `latest`: it
# moves on every main build and can jump mid-upgrade. An exact version (0.18.2) pins
# harder and never moves.
#
# A line always resolves to the newest patch published on it, so pinning the line and
# pinning its newest patch select the same image until the next patch ships — at which
# point the line follows and the exact version does not. That is the whole difference
# between them, and it is why the line is the one to pin unless you need to hold still.
#APP_IMAGE_TAG=0.18

# ── Optional: email (SMTP) ─────────────────────────────────────────

# NOT REQUIRED TO INSTALL. With no SMTP the instance starts and you sign in as the seeded
# administrator; you add further people in /admin, which hands you a one-time setup link to
# pass on yourself and sends no mail. Configure SMTP when you want verification, invite and
# password-reset mail to be delivered.
#
# These lines are COMMENTED rather than empty on purpose: an empty `MAIL_HOST=` is delivered
# to the container as an empty string and overrides the application's own default, while a
# commented line delivers nothing and lets the default stand.
#MAIL_HOST=
#MAIL_PORT=587
#MAIL_USERNAME=
#MAIL_PASSWORD=
#MAIL_SMTP_AUTH=true
#MAIL_STARTTLS=true
#MAIL_FROM=

# ── Optional: host sizing ──────────────────────────────────────────

# The app limit is not a tuning knob you can ignore: the image sizes its heap at 50% of the
# CONTAINER limit, so removing it sizes the heap against host RAM.
#APP_MEMORY_LIMIT=1g
#POSTGRES_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
#POSTGRES_SHARED_BUFFERS=128MB
#POSTGRES_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE=512MB
#POSTGRES_WORK_MEM=4MB
#POSTGRES_SHM_SIZE=64m
# Seconds between SIGTERM and SIGKILL. The app spends up to 15s flushing queued mail at
# shutdown and refuses to start if that would not fit.
#APP_STOP_GRACE_SECONDS=30

# Do NOT set SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE here. This stack is the self-hosted (dc) model and
# docker-compose.yml fixes the profile as a literal, which wins over this file.
#
# THAT PROTECTS THE PROFILE, NOT THE FILE. If you crib lines from .env.prod.example, the
# profile stays `dc` — but everything else in that file reaches the container verbatim, so
# STORAGE_TYPE=s3 or PUBLIC_SIGNUP_ENABLED=true WILL take effect here. Copy the lines you
# need, not the file.
#
# One more, for whoever edits the stack rather than this file: APP_PORT, APP_BIND,
# APP_IMAGE_TAG and APP_MEMORY_LIMIT are read by Docker Compose and by nothing in the
# application. They follow the APP_* convention because they belong to this app's stack, but
# Spring's relaxed binding would map them onto `app.port`, `app.bind` and so on the day
# anyone adds an @ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app") component with matching names —
# pre-set, on every install, from a file whose author never meant them as app settings.
